## Description
Ajv is vulnerable to regular expression denial-of-service (DoS) due to insufficient pattern validation when `$data: true` configuration is enabled. This issue could allow a remote attacker to render the application unresponsive by supplying a maliciously crafted regular expression and input.

## Vulnerabilities(BDSA,CVE)
:yellow_circle: [BDSA-2026-2425](https://openhub.net/vulnerabilities/bdsa/BDSA-2026-2425)([CVE-2025-69873](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2025-69873)) - Incorrect Regular Expression


## Solution
Fixed in [**6.14.0**](https://github.com/ajv-validator/ajv/releases/tag/v6.14.0) by [this](https://github.com/ajv-validator/ajv/commit/b552ed66191eb338498df3196065c777e3bb71f2) commit, and [**8.18.0**](https://github.com/ajv-validator/ajv/releases/tag/v8.18.0) by [this](https://github.com/ajv-validator/ajv/commit/720a23fa453ffae8340e92c9b0fe886c54cfe0d5) commit.

The latest stable releases can be found [here](https://github.com/ajv-validator/ajv).

## Workaround
The reporter of this vulnerability has suggested the following workarounds [here](https://github.com/EthanKim88/ethan-cve-disclosures/blob/main/CVE-2025-69873-ajv-ReDoS.md):
1. Avoid `$data` with the `pattern` keyword: If possible, use static patterns instead of dynamic ones. This eliminates the attack surface entirely.

2. Pre-validate patterns: If you must use dynamic patterns, validate them with `safe-regex` before they reach ajv:
```
const safeRegex = require('safe-regex');

